## Problem
Currently `updateFromState(state)` does full replacement:
```ts
function updateFromState(state: DocumentState) {
  nodes = state.nodes;  // Always new array
}
```

This provides no information about *what* changed, forcing full rebuilds.

## Proposed: Track change deltas
Option A: Backend returns change info
```ts
interface DocumentUpdate {
  nodes: Node[];
  changedIds?: string[];   // IDs that were modified
  deletedIds?: string[];   // IDs that were removed
  addedIds?: string[];     // IDs that were added
}
```

Option B: Frontend diffs the arrays
```ts
function updateFromState(state: DocumentState) {
  const oldIds = new Set(nodes.map(n => n.id));
  const newIds = new Set(state.nodes.map(n => n.id));

  const deleted = [...oldIds].filter(id => !newIds.has(id));
  const added = [...newIds].filter(id => !oldIds.has(id));

  // Now we know exactly what changed
  invalidateCacheFor(deleted, added);
  nodes = state.nodes;
}
```

Option C: Optimistic local updates
For known operations (delete, add, move), update local state first without waiting for API:
```ts
async deleteNode(nodeId) {
  // Immediate local update
  const deletedNode = nodesById().get(nodeId);
  nodes = nodes.filter(n => n.id !== nodeId);
  invalidateSubtreeCache(deletedNode.parent_id);

  // Then sync with backend
  await api.deleteNode(nodeId);
}
```

## Recommendation
Start with Option B (frontend diff) - least invasive, no backend changes.
Consider Option C for perceived performance (feels instant).

