- React component: `app/src/components/OutlineItem.tsx` (line 12 imports, never uses)
- React suggestion: `app/src/components/ui/WikiLinkSuggestion.tsx` (ready to use)
- Svelte reference: `app/src/lib/OutlineItem.svelte` (lines 43, 231, 1446)

Standard text formatting shortcuts must work in the TipTap editor and not be intercepted by app-level handlers.

## Standard Editor Shortcuts (must work)
| Shortcut | Action |
|----------|--------|
| Ctrl+B | Bold |
| Ctrl+I | Italic |
| Ctrl+U | Underline |
| Ctrl+K | Insert link |
| Ctrl+` | Inline code |
| Ctrl+Z | Undo (editor-level) |
| Ctrl+Y | Redo (editor-level) |

## App-Level Shortcuts (must NOT conflict)
| Shortcut | Action |
|----------|--------|
| Ctrl+Shift+I | Quick capture modal ✓ (no conflict with Ctrl+I) |
| Ctrl+Shift+O | Toggle sidebar (not Ctrl+B) |

## Potential Issues
1. App-level `handleKeyDown` intercepting before TipTap gets the event
2. `event.preventDefault()` called too early
3. Key handler not checking if editor is focused

## Fix Pattern
```tsx
// In App.tsx global handler
const handleKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
  // Let editor handle its own shortcuts when focused
  if (document.activeElement?.closest('.outline-editor')) {
    // Only intercept non-editor shortcuts
    if (\!isEditorShortcut(e)) {
      // handle app shortcuts
    }
    return;
  }
  // ... rest of app shortcuts
};
```

## Related
- otl-w6qh: Ctrl+B conflict with sidebar

## Files
- App keyboard handler: `app/src/App.tsx`
- Editor keyboard handler: `app/src/components/OutlineItem.tsx`

## Summary
Nearly every async method in `outline.svelte.ts` (30+ occurrences) follows this pattern:

```typescript
startOperation();
try {
  // ...
} catch (e) {
  error = e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e);
  return false;
} finally {
  endOperation();
}
```

## Solution
Create a wrapper function:

```typescript
async function withOperation<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T | null> {
  startOperation();
  try {
    return await fn();
  } catch (e) {
    error = e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e);
    return null;
  } finally {
    endOperation();
  }
}
```

This would eliminate ~100 lines of repetitive error handling code.

## Summary
The `handleKeyDown` function in `OutlineItem.svelte:309-650` is 340+ lines long, handling all keyboard shortcuts inline.

## Solution
Extract keyboard shortcuts into a mapping object or separate module:

```typescript
const keyboardShortcuts = {
  'Enter': { handler: handleEnter, requiresMeta: false },
  'Tab': { handler: handleIndent, requiresMeta: false },
  'Backspace': { handler: handleBackspace, requiresMeta: false },
  // ...
};
```

This improves maintainability and makes shortcuts easier to discover/modify.
